{"kind":"request","text":"Export a class for each refusal reason, so a client does not keep its own lists of which refusals are final.\n\nFiled 2026-11-13 07:43 EST at Hugh's request, from the dap-foldkit M2 reviews. Every dap fact below is read at main `64112e45`.\n\n## Ask\n\nBeside `REFUSAL_REASONS` in `packages/bytes/src/records.ts`, export a table that gives each `RefusalReason` one class. The table has the type `Record\u003cRefusalReason, RefusalClass\u003e`, so a new reason without a class fails to compile. Three classes cover what a client must decide:\n\n- **never**: the reason holds at every later head for the same signed bytes. These bytes can never take effect.\n- **head**: the reason speaks for the head that the scope judged at. The same bytes can take effect at a later head, until `notAfter`.\n- **protocol**: the client and the scope disagree on the definition or on the address. The client stops writing to this scope.\n\nFor each reason of class **never**, state the property that makes it hold, and test it: a judge that refuses bytes with that reason refuses them with that reason at every later head.\n\n## Why\n\nThe scope indexes acceptance and keeps no record of a refusal. The M0 probe of dap-foldkit showed bytes that the scope refused `no-item` at one head and accepted at a later head. A client that reads every refusal as final shows \"refused\" for an act that the record can still take.\n\nThree places decide this today, each by hand:\n\n- The dap-foldkit outbox has two string sets, `DEFINITIVE` (six reasons) and `OUT_OF_DATE` (five reasons), at `~/play/dap-foldkit/lib/outbox/outbox.ts:158-160`. I chose their members by reading the judge. `revision-moved`, `final` and `expired` are in `DEFINITIVE` because a revision only rises, a final state has no exit and the clock does not go back. No dap document states those three properties.\n- `Custody` reads every `refused` as a resolution of `unknown` (`packages/client/src/custody.ts:31-34`, `:404`).\n- `packages/client/src/request-dispatch.ts` keeps a `RefusalJudgment` for each attempt.\n\n`RefusalReason` has 38 members in `REFUSAL_REASONS`. A client author cannot class them without reading the judge.\n\n## Size and priority\n\nSmall to medium: one table, one type, and one property test for each reason of class **never**. Medium priority. The design note on the client primitive (the sibling request filed with this one) needs the classes for its custody rule.\n\n## Filing rule\n\nThe request adds no scope kind, no platform definition name, no capability, no tool family and no record model. It exports a table over a union that is already public. It changes no answer.\n\nSource: `~/play/dap-foldkit/PLAN.md`, draft 3, revision 6, section 8.3, row D11.","body":{"conditions":"A table of type Record\u003cRefusalReason, RefusalClass\u003e is exported beside REFUSAL_REASONS, so a reason with no class fails to compile. Each reason of the class that never takes effect has a stated property and a test that the same bytes stay refused at a later head. Custody and the browser dispatch read the table or the planner records why not. npm run gate passes.","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}